  "image_name": "7d5c557b2b5304c20791d9105fd4e375.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how dune plants both shape coastal dunes and respond to dune conditions. In the main diagram, grasses growing on a sandy dune slow the wind, trap sand, and reduce erosion, so vegetation helps build and stabilize the dune. Their roots and rhizomes extend deep and laterally through the sand, anchoring shoots, binding sediment, and adding dune volume and carbon storage. At the same time, plant growth is controlled by coastal stresses: waves, sea water, salt spray, sand burial, and wind-driven sand can lower productivity, while marine nutrients from wrack and sand may increase it. The drawing also shows the water table rising beneath the dune, suggesting that moisture conditions and distance from groundwater affect plant performance.\n\nThe lower section compares several dune plant types with species-specific forms. It shows clear differences in height, aboveground growth pattern, and underground architecture. Some species are taller and densely tufted, others are shorter or more spreading; root systems also vary from deeper vertical structures to more horizontal rhizomes. Overall, the comparison emphasizes that different dune plants likely influence dune building and stabilization in different ways because of their contrasting shapes and root networks.",
